  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">SPVO &#x2013; Celestial Cacao</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Currently, coffee and cacao farmers in the Sierra Nevada de Santa Marta are facing the consequences of climate change, affecting their crops and livelihoods. Quality coffee requires altitude and a cool climate at night. But, since the local climate has changed, pests and diseases are affecting the lower altitude coffee regions. These regions are now better suited for cultivating cacao.The beneficiary farmers&apos; cooperative Anei, is responding to this change by helping their farmers to switch from coffee to cacao. Especially in the lower unproductive regions, in order to build a stable livelihood.Most farmers will be from Arhuaco, descent, but can also be from other indigenous groups or non-indigenous. The Arhauco community has its own reserve in the area.The project &quot;Celestial Cacao&quot; focusses on 200 families, 1,000 beneficiaries, on 375 hectares of land. The lands will be rehabilitated, if not productive enough for coffee, or/and already deforested lands will be added to turn into cacao agroforestry systems. The cacao production will enable farmers to earn a living income, which is the aim of the project. The project also aims to work on more biodiversity through agroforestry systems with cacao.Chocolatemakers, a single origin organic chocolate company based in Amsterdam, is the project lead. They source cacao beans from Anei and produce about high-quality chocolate that is good for people and planet.</narrative>
  </description>
